{
  "dom_id": "step-q108573",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/7c627af4c6fdff2577a0114c5ab84444041e5e1ef53d2e717912dd130b197be7.png)\n\nWhich of the polyhedra above are convex?",
  "worked_solution": "A polyhedron is convex if and only if the following condition holds:\n\n*If we take any two distinct points $A$ and $B$ that belong to the polyhedron, then all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ must also belong to the polyhedron.*  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our solids in turn.\n\n- Solid I is *not* convex. The above condition does not hold. As a counterexample, let's consider the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/a8dc22e973680034ef5013ef6fb5e37043559d830bfcc50fbb6a7a81775e299b.png)\n\n Notice that some points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ lie outside the solid.\n\n- Solids II and III are convex. Indeed, the above condition holds for any two points that belong to the solids.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
